Ansible Blocks

Today I learned about Ansible Blocks, which are used to group tasks and handle errors in a structured way 🧱
Blocks help in organizing tasks and provide built-in error recovery mechanisms.
 This makes Playbooks more reliable and production-ready ⚙️

🔹 📦 block
A block is used to group multiple tasks together under one section.
This is useful when:
 • Tasks are logically related
 • Common conditions apply
 • Error handling is needed for a group of tasks
It improves readability and better task organization 📘

🔹 🚑 rescue
The rescue section runs only if a task inside the block fails.
It acts like an error recovery mechanism.
 Instead of stopping the Playbook, Ansible executes the rescue tasks to handle failures gracefully 🔄
This is very useful in real-world automation where failures are expected.

🔹 🔁 always
The always section runs no matter what happens — success or failure.
Common use cases:
 • Cleanup tasks
 • Logging
 • Notifications
 • Final checks
This ensures important steps are never skipped ⚠️

🔹 🛠️ Error Recovery
Using block + rescue + always together provides powerful error handling.
Workflow:
 • Try main tasks (block)
 • If failure occurs → run rescue
 • Finally → run always tasks
This approach increases automation reliability and fault tolerance 🚀

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Blocks group related tasks logically.
 Rescue handles failures gracefully.
 Always ensures critical tasks run every time.
